<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter10_enumerations-and-enum-class">
    <title>Enumerations and <c>enum class</c></title>

    <p>
        Many programs need to represent one choice from a limited set of
        possibilities. A traffic light can be red, yellow, or green. A task can
        be not started, active, or completed. An enumeration gives each choice
        a meaningful name while keeping the choices within one type.
    </p>

    <p>
        C++ provides the older unscoped <c>enum</c> and the modern scoped
        <c>enum class</c>. We will study both because students will encounter
        traditional enumerations in existing code, but we will generally prefer
        <c>enum class</c> in new C++17 code.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_enumeration-finite-choices">
        <title>Enumerations Represent Named Choices</title>

        <p>
            A traditional enumeration lists a set of named enumerators. If no
            values are specified, C++ normally assigns consecutive integer
            values beginning with zero.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_traditional-enum">
            <title>A traditional enumeration for traffic-light choices</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_traditional-enum"
                     language="cpp">
                <code><![CDATA[
enum TrafficLight {
    red,       // 0
    yellow,    // 1
    green      // 2
};

TrafficLight light = green;
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The names <c>red</c>, <c>yellow</c>, and <c>green</c> are called
            <term>enumerators</term>. They are named constants associated with
            the enumeration. The underlying integer values exist, but the names
            communicate the program's meaning more clearly than unexplained
            numbers.
        </p>

        <p>
            Traditional enumerators are placed in the surrounding scope. For
            example, the name <c>red</c> can be used directly after the
            declaration. This convenient behavior can also cause name conflicts
            when different enumerations use the same enumerator names.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_enum-class-scope">
        <title>Scoped Enumerations with <c>enum class</c></title>

        <p>
            A scoped enumeration places its enumerators inside the enumeration's
            own scope. The enumeration name must be written before the
            enumerator:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter10_enum-class-scope"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
enum class TrafficLight {
    red,
    yellow,
    green
};

TrafficLight light = TrafficLight::green;
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter10_enum-comparison">
            <title>Traditional <c>enum</c> and scoped <c>enum class</c></title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="37%"/>
                <col width="38%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Feature</cell>
                    <cell>Traditional <c>enum</c></cell>
                    <cell><c>enum class</c></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Enumerator use</cell>
                    <cell><c>green</c></cell>
                    <cell><c>TrafficLight::green</c></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Enumerator scope</cell>
                    <cell>Surrounding scope</cell>
                    <cell>Inside the enumeration</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Implicit conversion to <c>int</c></cell>
                    <cell>Allowed</cell>
                    <cell>Not allowed</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Name-conflict protection</cell>
                    <cell>Less protection</cell>
                    <cell>Stronger protection</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Recommended for new code</cell>
                    <cell>Usually no</cell>
                    <cell>Usually yes</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The stronger type checking of <c>enum class</c> prevents accidental
            mixing of unrelated enumerations and prevents an enumeration value
            from silently becoming an integer. This is why the scoped form is
            usually the better choice for new code.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_enum-integer-values">
        <title>Explicit Integer Values</title>

        <p>
            A program can assign explicit integer values when the values have
            meaning outside the program, such as protocol codes, menu codes, or
            priority levels.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_enum-explicit-values">
            <title>Named priority values with explicit integers</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_enum-explicit-values"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

enum class Priority : int {
    low = 1,
    normal = 5,
    urgent = 10
};

int main() {
    Priority task_priority = Priority::urgent;
    int numeric_priority = static_cast<int>(task_priority);

    std::cout << numeric_priority << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The values in this example are integer constants associated with the
            enumerators. The declaration also specifies <c>int</c> as the
            underlying type. Because <c>Priority</c> is an <c>enum class</c>,
            C++ does not silently convert <c>Priority::urgent</c> to an
            integer. The explicit <c>static_cast&lt;int&gt;</c> documents that the
            program intentionally wants the numeric value.
        </p>

        <p>
            Prefer comparing and passing the enumeration value itself when
            possible. Convert to an integer only when an interface specifically
            requires a numeric code.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_enum-switch">
        <title>Using Enumerations with <c>switch</c></title>

        <p>
            An enumeration works naturally with <c>switch</c>. Each case names
            one meaningful choice instead of repeating a numeric code.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_enum-switch">
            <title>Describing a traffic-light state</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_enum-switch"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

enum class TrafficLight {
    red,
    yellow,
    green
};

std::string describe_light(TrafficLight light) {
    switch (light) {
        case TrafficLight::red:
            return "Stop";
        case TrafficLight::yellow:
            return "Prepare";
        case TrafficLight::green:
            return "Go";
    }

    return "Unknown";
}

int main() {
    TrafficLight light = TrafficLight::yellow;

    std::cout << describe_light(light) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The enumeration does not automatically become a string. The
            <c>describe_light()</c> function explicitly translates each named
            choice into text. A final return statement provides a safe fallback
            if a value outside the listed cases is ever produced.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_enum-class-state">
        <title>Enumerations as Class State</title>

        <p>
            An enumeration can represent the state of an object. Keeping the
            enumeration private prevents outside code from assigning arbitrary
            states directly; public member functions can define the permitted
            transitions.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_enum-task-state">
            <title>Representing task state inside a class</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_enum-task-state"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Task {
private:
    enum class Status {
        not_started,
        active,
        completed
    };

    Status status = Status::not_started;

public:
    void start_task() {
        status = Status::active;
    }

    void complete_task() {
        status = Status::completed;
    }

    bool is_completed() const {
        return status == Status::completed;
    }
};

int main() {
    Task task;

    task.start_task();
    task.complete_task();

    std::cout << task.is_completed() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The outside code does not need to know the enumeration's internal
            names to use the task. It calls behavior-oriented member functions,
            while the class protects the representation of its state.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_enum-codelens">
        <title>Tracing an Enumeration and Its Integer Value</title>

        <p>
            The visualization shows an <c>enum class</c> value, its explicit
            integer value, and the branch selected by a <c>switch</c> statement.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter10_enumeration"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aenum%20class%20Priority%20%3A%20int%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20low%20%3D%201%2C%0A%20%20%20%20normal%20%3D%205%2C%0A%20%20%20%20urgent%20%3D%2010%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Priority%20priority%20%3D%20Priority%3A%3Aurgent%3B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20numeric_priority%20%3D%20static_cast%3Cint%3E%28priority%29%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20switch%20%28priority%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20case%20Priority%3A%3Alow%3A%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Low%22%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20break%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20case%20Priority%3A%3Anormal%3A%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Normal%22%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20break%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20case%20Priority%3A%3Aurgent%3A%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Urgent%20%28%22%20%3C%3C%20numeric_priority%20%3C%3C%20%22%29%22%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20break%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=560&amp;codeDivWidth=450&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;mode=display&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="4:3">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter10_enum_exercise-purpose">
        <statement>
            <p>What is the main purpose of an enumeration?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>To represent one choice from a limited set of named possibilities.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Enumerations give meaningful names to a fixed collection of alternatives.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>To store an unlimited number of strings.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A string or container is more appropriate for an unlimited collection of text.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>To make every integer variable constant.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>An enumeration defines a particular set of named values.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>To replace every class in a program.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Enumerations model choices; classes model objects with state and behavior.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_enum_exercise-scope">
        <statement>
            <p>Which expression correctly uses the <c>green</c> enumerator from <c>enum class TrafficLight</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p><c>TrafficLight::green</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Scoped enumerators are accessed through the enumeration name and <c>::</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>green</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>That unqualified form belongs to a traditional unscoped enumeration.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>TrafficLight.green</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The dot operator accesses an object member; enumerators use <c>::</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p><c>enum_class::TrafficLight::green</c></p></statement>
                <feedback><p><c>enum class</c> is declaration syntax, not part of the access expression.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_enum_parsons-switch" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the statements to declare a scoped enumeration, select a value, and handle it with <c>switch</c>.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>enum class Priority { low, normal, urgent };</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>Priority priority = Priority::urgent;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>switch (priority) {</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>    case Priority::urgent:</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>        std::cout &lt;&lt; "Urgent" &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="6"><cline>        break;</cline></block>
            <block order="7"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_enum_exercise-integer-values">
        <statement>
            <p>What does <c>static_cast&lt;int&gt;(Priority::urgent)</c> do when <c>urgent = 10</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>It explicitly obtains the integer value <c>10</c>.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The cast documents the intentional conversion from the scoped enumeration value to its underlying integer representation.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It changes the enumeration's name to <c>int</c>.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The cast converts one value; it does not change the enumeration declaration.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It creates a new enumeration named <c>int</c>.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A cast performs a value conversion, not a type declaration.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It changes <c>urgent</c> from 10 to zero.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The explicit value remains 10.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_enum_exercise-type-safety">
        <statement>
            <p>Why is <c>enum class</c> usually preferred for new C++ code?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Its enumerators are scoped and it does not implicitly convert to <c>int</c>.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Scoped names and stronger type checking prevent several accidental uses.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It automatically converts every value to a string.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Text conversion still requires explicit code such as a function with <c>switch</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It can contain any number of values at runtime.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>An enumeration still represents a declared set of named choices.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It removes the need for constructors in classes.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Enumerations and constructors solve different design problems.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</section>
